 ## Overview
-Predicted Normal Loss is a regularization term in differentiable volume rendering that enforces geometric consistency by aligning explicitly predicted surface normals with normals derived from the scene's density field [S2]. It addresses normal estimation ambiguity in radiance fields by ensuring the learned geometry and appearance features describe the same surface structure [S2]. Unlike sibling methods that enforce smoothness or ray alignment, this loss couples the explicit normal prediction head to the implicit density gradient [S2].
+Predicted Normal Loss is a regularization term in differentiable volume rendering that enforces geometric consistency by aligning explicitly predicted surface normals with normals derived from the scene's density field [S2]. It addresses normal estimation ambiguity in radiance fields by ensuring the learned geometry and appearance features describe the same surface structure [S2]. Unlike sibling methods such as [Curvature Loss](2911-curvature-loss.md) or [Normal Ray Alignment Loss](4202-normal-ray-alignment-loss.md) that enforce smoothness or ray alignment, this loss couples the explicit normal prediction head to the implicit density gradient [S2]. One source defines the loss as supervising predicted normals against rendered normals [S1, unofficial], whereas the standard formulation constrains them against the negative density gradient [S2].
 
